    "lawCode": "Amd §§603-a & 1194, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5562A             REVISED MEMO 06/09/2017\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to mandatory testing in the event of a motor vehicle\ncollision resulting in injury or death\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo provide a mechanism for greater enforcement of driving while\nintoxicated and driving while ability impaired laws involving motor\nvehicle accidents.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill amends Subdivision one of section 603-a of\nthe Vehicle and Traffic Law to require that police officers present at\nand investigating a vehicle accident where a person was seriously\ninjured or killed request that all operators of motor vehicles\ninvolved in the accident submit to a field sobriety test provided that\nthere are reasonable grounds to believe that such motor vehicle\noperator committed a serious traffic violation in the same accident.\nRefusal of this test makes that operator subject to the provisions\nincluded in section 1194 of the Vehicle and Traffic Law.\n\nSection two of this bill amends paragraphs (b) and (c) of Subdivision\ntwo of section 1194 of the Vehicle and Traffic Law. Paragraph (b) is\namended regarding the requirements for a police officer's report where\nthere are reasonable grounds to believe that an individual committed a\nserious traffic violation that resulted in serious injury or death and\nthat individual refused field sobriety and chemical BAC tests. It also\nis amended so that those individuals who are arrested or issued a\nsummons for a serious traffic violation committed in the same accident\nwhere a person was seriously injured or killed, will have their\nlicense or permit to drive temporarily suspended.  Paragraph (c) is\namended with regard to the hearings permitted by law for those who\nhave had their license suspended pursuant to the provisions in\nparagraph (b) of subdivision two of section 1194 of the vehicle and\ntraffic law.\n\nSection three of this bill states that this act shall take effect on\nthe thirtieth day after it shall have become law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAll too often, intoxicated drivers who are involved in motor vehicle\ncrashes escape prosecution. New York State has been found to test a\nsmall amount of surviving drivers who were involved in fatal crashes\nfor their blood alcohol concentration. Almost 1/4 of all drivers in\nfatal crashes are impaired by alcohol. Only two states reported\ntesting a smaller percentage of surviving drivers involved in fatal\ncrashes. This legislation requires a.mandatory chemical test be\nperformed when there is a serious personal injury or a death as a\nresult of a motor vehicle accident.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2005-2006: A.7002-A referred to Codes\n2007-2008: S.4014 referred to Transportation/A.6945 referred to Codes\n2009-2010: S.2498 referred to Transportation/A.3810 referred to Codes\n2012: S.4422 passed Senate/A.2645 referred to Codes\n2014: A.8560A passed Assembly\n2015/2016 S.8051 passed Senate/A.7890 referred to Transportation\n\n FISCAL IMPACT :\n\nNone.\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
